UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 23: Playbuilding', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change. Peer feedback is for the work, spoken or written so the person can use it. Kindness here is precise, not vague praise and not a comment about someone''s body, clothes, voice type, or worth. An acting choice is a decision the audience could see, such as a pause, a point, or which way a pocket opens. A suggestion is the next change, also visible. Useful note: ''The pause before you opened the pocket made the key feel important. Next time, open the pocket toward the front row so we can see it is empty.'' That keeps one choice and asks for one change. ''You were great'' and ''You are bad at drama'' both fail, because neither says what to repeat or repair. Write exactly those two parts. Watching a live try is optional; you can respond to a written storyboard. Any shared work needs consent and an adult nearby.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA classmate paused before opening the empty pocket, but the pocket faced the floor. Which peer note is kind and specific?\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row. The pause is the acting choice worth repeating. The pocket''s direction is the suggested change. The note talks about the work. It does not score the person. ''That was amazing'' keeps nothing particular, and ''You can''t act'' suggests nothing they can try.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSaying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change. General praise feels kind and still gives the actor no next step. A comment about a person''s body, clothes, or worth is not feedback on the scene. Repair the note by keeping one visible choice and asking for one visible change.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite one kind note about waiting at a closed canteen shutter: one choice, one change. The student stops, looks at the shutter, and then speaks to a friend while still facing away from the row. Name one acting choice to keep and one suggested change. Do not comment on bodies, clothes, or worth. Do not use the house key. The written note is the evidence.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Drama","learning_intention":"Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change. Peer feedback is for the work, spoken or written so the person can use it. Kindness here is precise, not vague praise and not a comment about someone''s body, clothes, voice type, or worth. An acting choice is a decision the audience could see, such as a pause, a point, or which way a pocket opens. A suggestion is the next change, also visible. Useful note: ''The pause before you opened the pocket made the key feel important. Next time, open the pocket toward the front row so we can see it is empty.'' That keeps one choice and asks for one change. ''You were great'' and ''You are bad at drama'' both fail, because neither says what to repeat or repair. Write exactly those two parts. Watching a live try is optional; you can respond to a written storyboard. Any shared work needs consent and an adult nearby.","lesson_number":23,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DR4-MAK-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write one kind note about waiting at a closed canteen shutter: one choice, one change. The student stops, looks at the shutter, and then speaks to a friend while still facing away from the row. Name one acting choice to  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using peer feedback on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change.. Correction: Keep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change. General praise feels kind and still gives the actor no next step. A comment about a person''s body, clothes, or worth is not feedback on the scene. Repair the note by keeping one visible choice and asking for one visible change. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating peer feedback on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with peer feedback, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about peer feedback with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for peer feedback. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for peer feedback. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","Keep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row","Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change"],"question":"A classmate paused before opening the empty pocket, but the pocket faced the floor. Which peer note is kind and specific?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for peer feedback; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of acting choice with clear evidence","Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change","Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change","Keep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named acting choice, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply peer feedback to new material and name the acting choice used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change","Name acting choice only, without linking it to peer feedback"],"question":"Which response best applies peer feedback with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on peer feedback. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change","Keep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for peer feedback"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","The pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name acting choice only, with no linked example","Write one kind note about waiting at a closed canteen shutter: one choice, one change","Retype the worked example with no new example","Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change"],"question":"Which independent response best applies peer feedback to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about peer feedback with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating peer feedback on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using peer feedback on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for peer feedback. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with peer feedback, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change.. Correction: Keep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-drama-y9-w16-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 Drama 7-10 (2023) alignment to DR4-MAK-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise peer feedback.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use peer feedback with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify acting choice and explain why it matters.","I can practise suggestion independently on a new prompt.","I can notice kindness needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row. Choice to keep: Riley waits a breath before opening the zip pocket, so the loss feels serious. Change to try: angle the open pocket to the front row, because last time the lining faced the floor and the empty look was missed. The note does not mention height, clothes, or whether someone is ''a natural''. A second example, still kind: ''Alex''s point at the bench told me who had found the ring. Hold the point until the freeze, because it dropped too soon.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nA classmate paused before opening the empty pocket, but the pocket faced the floor. Which peer note is kind and specific?\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row. The pause is the acting choice worth repeating. The pocket''s direction is the suggested change. The note talks about the work. It does not score the person. ''That was amazing'' keeps nothing particular, and ''You can''t act'' suggests nothing they can try.","explain":"Kind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change. Peer feedback is for the work, spoken or written so the person can use it. Kindness here is precise, not vague praise and not a comment about someone''s body, clothes, voice type, or worth. An acting choice is a decision the audience could see, such as a pause, a point, or which way a pocket opens. A suggestion is the next change, also visible. Useful note: ''The pause before you opened the pocket made the key feel important. Next time, open the pocket toward the front row so we can see it is empty.'' That keeps one choice and asks for one change. ''You were great'' and ''You are bad at drama'' both fail, because neither says what to repeat or repair. Write exactly those two parts. Watching a live try is optional; you can respond to a written storyboard. Any shared work needs consent and an adult nearby.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change. General praise feels kind and still gives the actor no next step. A comment about a person''s body, clothes, or worth is not feedback on the scene. Repair the note by keeping one visible choice and asking for one visible change. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Feedback that keeps a pause beside a suggestion to open the pocket toward the audience.","caption":"Name one acting choice and one specific change.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Choice to keep</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The pause showed worry</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Change to try</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Open pocket to the row</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: The pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row. Choice to keep: Riley waits a breath  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: The pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row. Choice to keep: Riley waits a breath  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row. Choice to keep: Riley waits a breath before opening the zip pocket, so the loss feels serious. Change to try: angle the open pocket to the front row, because last time the lining faced the floor and the empty look was missed. The note does not mention height, clothes, or whether someone is ''a natural''. A second example, still kind: ''Alex''s point at the bench told me who had found the ring. Hold the point until the freeze, because it dropped too soon.'' Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A note about a late point contrasted with a comment about the person.","caption":"Stay kind, and talk about the choice.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">About the work</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The point landed late</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">About the person</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">You are bad at drama</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSaying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change. General praise feels kind and still gives the actor no next step. A comment about a person''s body, clothes, or worth is not feedback on the scene. Repair the note by keeping one visible choice and asking for one visible change. Here is a more helpful example: The pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row. Choice to keep: Riley waits a breath before opening the zip pocket, so the loss feels serious. Change to try: angle the open pocket to the front row, because last time the lining faced the floor and the empty look was missed. The note does not mention height, clothes, or whether someone is ''a natural''. A second example, still kind: ''Alex''s point at the bench told me who had found the ring. Hold the point until the freeze, because it dropped too soon.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nKeep the pause that showed worry, and open the pocket toward the front row. The pause is the acting choice worth repeating. The pocket''s direction is the suggested change. The note talks about the work. It does not score the person. ''That was amazing'' keeps nothing particular, and ''You can''t act'' suggests nothing they can try.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Saying someone was great, or bad at drama, names no choice to keep or change. General praise feels kind and still gives the actor no next step. A comment about a person''s body, clothes, or worth is not feedback on the scene. Repair the note by keeping one visible choice and asking for one visible change. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nKind feedback names one acting choice and one specific change. Peer feedback is for the work, spoken or written so the person can use it. Kindness here is precise, not vague praise and not a comment about someone''s body, clothes, voice type, or worth. An acting choice is a decision the audience could see, such as a pause, a point, or which way a pocket opens. A suggestion is the next change, also visible. Useful note: ''The pause before you opened the pocket made the key feel important. Next time, open the pocket toward the front row so we can see it is empty.'' That keeps one choice and asks for one change. ''You were great'' and ''You are bad at drama'' both fail, because neither says what to repeat or repair. Write exactly those two parts. Watching a live try is optional; you can respond to a written storyboard. Any shared work needs consent and an adult nearby. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 23: Playbuilding","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Playbuilding","week":18,"week_in_unit":6,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nThe pause before the pocket shows worry, and the pocket should face the front row. Choice to keep: Riley waits a breath before opening the zip pocket, so the loss feels serious. Change to try: angle the open pocket to the front row, because last time the lining faced the floor and the empty look was missed. The note does not mention height, clothes, or whether someone is ''a natural''. A second example, still kind: ''Alex''s point at the bench told me who had found the ring. Hold the point until the freeze, because it dropped too soon.''\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Feedback that keeps a pause beside a suggestion to open the pocket toward the audience.","caption":"Name one acting choice and one specific change.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Choice to keep</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The pause showed worry</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Change to try</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Open pocket to the row</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A note about a late point contrasted with a comment about the person.","caption":"Stay kind, and talk about the choice.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">About the work</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The point landed late</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">About the person</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">You are bad at drama</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-drama-y9-w16-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
